SECTION "Data Retention Policy"

  TEXT
    This document is written in DocLang's **strict** dialect: every block is
    declared, nothing is inferred, and the normalizer never runs. That is what
    makes it reviewable in a pull request and meaningful to sign off on — you
    can audit a declaration; you cannot audit an inference.

  :::info
  The flex dialect is the right tool for drafting. Strict is the right tool for
  the version that gets committed.
  :::

SECTION "Scope"
  level: 2
  id: scope

  TEXT
    The policy below applies to every record the platform stores on behalf of a
    customer, in any region.

  POINTS
    - Application databases and their replicas
    - Object storage buckets, including lifecycle-expired objects
    - Log aggregation and metrics pipelines

SECTION "Retention Windows"

  TEXT
    Retention is measured from the record's creation timestamp, not from last
    access. See \ref{tbl-windows} for the authoritative values.

  TABLE
    headers: ["Data class", "Retention", "Deletion mode"]
    rows: [
      ["Operational logs", "30 days", "Automatic"],
      ["Audit trail", "7 years", "Manual (dual approval)"],
      ["Customer content", "Contract term + 90 days", "Automatic"],
      ["Backups", "35 days", "Automatic"]
    ]
    caption: "Retention window by data class"
    label: tbl-windows

SECTION "Deletion Guarantees"
  level: 2
  id: guarantees

  TEXT
    Automatic deletion is idempotent and re-runs until it observes zero
    remaining objects.

  CODE bash
    # Verify that a retention sweep converged
    retention-cli verify --class operational-logs --expect-empty

  :::warning
  Manual deletion of the audit trail requires two approvers and is itself
  written to the audit trail. There is no path that removes the record of a
  removal.
  :::

SECTION "Open Questions"

  CHECKLIST
    - [x] Confirm the backup window with the storage vendor
